  <p>Every rule on this page was read at its source on October 18, 2026. Read the column on who each rule binds before anything else. The California law most often described as a labeling law binds the engine makers, not you. Only two rules put a label inside a brand's ad, and both are about synthetic performers: New York's, now, and California's, from January.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">No federal statute, FTC rule, FTC guide or FTC policy statement read for this page requires a label on AI-generated content in a commercial ad. The FTC works through its general ban on deception, its endorsement guidance and its fake review rule.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: 16 CFR Parts 255, 461 and 465, read through the eCFR (current to October 15, 2026), and the FTC's Federal Register documents since June 2025, checked October 18, 2026.</p>

  <h2>Does the FTC require a label on AI-generated ads?</h2>

  <p>No. What it requires is older and wider: an ad can't leave people with a false impression, however it was made.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Section 5 of the FTC Act declares "unfair or deceptive acts or practices in or affecting commerce" unlawful. The FTC reads an ad by its net impression, looking at "the entire mosaic, rather than each tile separately." Written disclosures or fine print "may be insufficient to correct a misleading representation."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: FTC Act, Section 5, 15 U.S.C. 45; FTC Policy Statement on Deception, October 14, 1983.</p>

  <p>An AI packshot that shows the product as it is raises no Section 5 question. An AI image that makes a serum look richer or a sofa deeper than the real thing does, and it would if a retoucher had done it. A label in the corner doesn't fix that. Fixing the picture does.</p>

  <p>Generated people bring in the Endorsement Guides. The FTC revised them in 2023, and its announcement listed virtual influencers among the topics. An endorser is now anyone who "could be or appear to be an individual, group, or institution."</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Endorsements "must reflect the honest opinions, findings, beliefs, or experience of the endorser." An ad that presents "actual consumers" should use actual consumers, or clearly and conspicuously disclose that it does not.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: FTC Guides Concerning the Use of Endorsements and Testimonials in Advertising, 16 CFR 255.1(a) and 255.2(c), effective July 26, 2023, read October 18, 2026.</p>

  <p>In production terms, a generated "customer" who says a cream changed her skin is a consumer endorsement from someone who never used it. Say it is not a real customer, or don't run it. A brand mascot nobody mistakes for a buyer is another matter.</p>

  <p>The Guides carry no penalties of their own. The FTC calls them administrative interpretations of the laws it enforces (in practice, how it reads Section 5 when a case comes).</p>

  <h2>What does the FTC's fake review rule say about AI?</h2>

  <p>It's the one federal rule that the FTC, in its own notice, says covers AI-made content. It also carries civil penalties, which the Guides don't.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The rule bars reviews and testimonials that misrepresent "that the reviewer or testimonialist exists." The FTC's announcement gave "AI-generated fake reviews" as an example of reviews by someone who does not exist, and the rule's Federal Register notice says AI-generated reviews are covered.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: FTC Trade Regulation Rule on the Use of Consumer Reviews and Testimonials, 16 CFR Part 465, announced August 14, 2024, effective October 21, 2024; 89 FR 68037, footnote 35.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A knowing violation of an FTC rule carries a civil penalty of up to 53,088 dollars per violation, an amount the FTC left unchanged for 2026.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: 16 CFR 1.98(d), as adjusted January 17, 2025; FTC notice of September 15, 2026, 91 FR 58446.</p>

  <p>The FTC has started using it.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">FTC staff sent warning letters to 10 companies about possible violations of the reviews rule in December 2025. A final order in July 2026, against a supplement seller over fake and employee-written reviews, required a payment of 750,000 dollars.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: FTC press releases of December 22, 2025, and July 15, 2026.</p>

  <p>For a brand making content with AI, that rules out a short list of shortcuts: generated reviews on a product page, a testimonial from a customer who doesn't exist, a quote card with an invented name and face. Each is a fake review, whatever tool wrote it.</p>

  <h2>What has the FTC actually enforced on AI?</h2>

  <p>Mostly claims about what AI can do. None of the cases read for this page turns on a missing label.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">In September 2024 the FTC announced five actions against "operations that use AI hype": one over the claimed abilities of an AI legal-services product, one against an AI tool that wrote reviews, and three against online store schemes sold as AI-powered income.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: FTC press release, September 25, 2024.</p>

  <p>The review-tool order didn't last. On December 22, 2025, the FTC reopened it and set it aside, saying it "unduly burdens innovation in the nascent AI industry." What the agency has kept pursuing is the overclaim: AI products said to do what they don't.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The FTC said a company promoted its AI content detector as "98 percent" accurate when independent testing put its accuracy on general-purpose content at 53 percent. The final order came in August 2025.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: FTC press releases of April 28 and August 28, 2025.</p>

  <p>Some federal pieces are still moving. The FTC's proposal to extend its impersonation rule from businesses and government to individuals, deepfakes included, has no final action; its regulatory notice of October 1, 2026, says the agency "is still evaluating its options."</p>

  <p>Under a December 2025 executive order, the FTC has also proposed, not issued, a policy statement on state laws that require changes to the truthful outputs of AI models. It is aimed at AI models, not at ad labels. A federal digital replica bill, the NO FAKES Act, sits in committee in both chambers. Nothing here changes what a brand has to label today.</p>

  <h2>What does New York's synthetic performer law require?</h2>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Anyone who produces or creates an ad for a product or service "shall conspicuously disclose in such advertisement that a synthetic performer is in such advertisement, where such person has actual knowledge." A first violation costs 1,000 dollars, and each later one 5,000 dollars.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: New York General Business Law section 396-b, as amended by S8420-A, chapter 617 of the Laws of 2025, signed December 11, 2025, in force June 9, 2026; bill text read October 18, 2026.</p>

  <p>A synthetic performer, in the statute's words, is a digitally created asset made with generative AI or a software algorithm to give the impression of a human performer "who is not recognizable as any identifiable natural performer." A generated model in a jacket is one. So is a generated presenter in a product video. A real model whose photo was retouched is not, because a real person performed.</p>

  <p>The law leaves out audio ads, ads for films, shows and games that use the performer the way the work does, and AI used only to translate a real performer. The newspapers, stations, streamers and billboards that carry an ad are excluded too. The duty sits with whoever made it.</p>

  <p>Actual knowledge is the hinge. Whoever chose to generate the performer knows, so the answer belongs in the brief and in the delivery note, where the brand and the media buyer will see it too.</p>

  <p>A bill filed in September 2026, A11683, would keep the disclosure on screen for as long as the performer appears in a video. It sits in committee.</p>

  <h2>What changes in California on January 1, 2027?</h2>

  <p>California adds its own synthetic performer rule. It reaches further than New York's in two places: audio, and the wording.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">From January 1, 2027, it is unlawful to publish an ad that "prominently includes a synthetic performer without a clear and conspicuous disclosure." The disclosure must use wording substantially similar to "this performance features a synthetic performer" or "no human performer is depicted."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: California Business and Professions Code section 17610, added by SB 1050, chapter 246 of the Statutes of 2026, approved September 16, 2026, in force January 1, 2027, under the state constitution's default date for regular-session statutes; chaptered text read October 18, 2026.</p>

