  <p>"Prominently" is defined. The synthetic performer is in the foreground showing the product, voices the narration or the message, or reacts to it. A generated crowd far behind a real presenter falls outside. A generated voice-over falls inside.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A violation of section 17610 is a violation of section 17500, a misdemeanor punishable by up to six months in county jail or a fine of up to 2,500 dollars, and section 17536 adds a civil penalty of up to 2,500 dollars per violation.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: California Business and Professions Code sections 17500, 17536 and 17610(f), code and chaptered text read October 18, 2026.</p>

  <h2>Does California require labels on AI-generated images?</h2>

  <p>Not from brands. The California AI Transparency Act, operative since August 2, 2026, puts its duties on the makers of generative AI systems, and from 2027 on large platforms.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Since September 30, 2026, any person that produces a generative AI system publicly accessible in California must, where technically feasible, embed a hidden disclosure in the image, video or audio the system creates or alters beyond minor edits. It names the provider, the system and its version, the time and date, a unique identifier, and whether the content was created or altered. Each violation costs 5,000 dollars, and every day counts as a separate violation.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: California Business and Professions Code sections 22757.1 to 22757.4, as amended by SB 1000, chapter 861 of the Statutes of 2026, an urgency statute in effect September 30, 2026; chaptered text read October 18, 2026.</p>

  <p>SB 1000 also scrapped two things many explainers still describe: the threshold of a million monthly users and the duty to offer users a visible label. From January 1, 2027, large online platforms must detect provenance data, show it, and not knowingly strip it. Camera makers follow in 2028.</p>

  <p>For a brand, the point is the file. What your engines produce is meant to carry a hidden record of its origin, and from January the big platforms have to read it. Keep it. An export step that strips metadata throws away the proof, and <a href="/resources/insights/content-credentials-c2pa-in-production">Content Credentials in a real pipeline</a> shows where that proof survives (and where it breaks).</p>

  <h2>Which other state laws touch AI in ads?</h2>

  <p>Consent laws, mostly. They ask for permission rather than a label, and no label stands in for permission.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Tennessee's ELVIS Act, in force since July 1, 2024, makes it actionable to use a person's voice or likeness to advertise without consent, and its definition of voice includes a simulation of it.</li>
    <li>Hawaii's Act 247, in force since July 14, 2026, makes it unlawful to knowingly publish a realistic AI imitation of an identifiable person in connection with an ad without that person's written consent.</li>
    <li>New York amended its Civil Rights Law on December 11, 2025: commercial use of a deceased performer's digital replica needs the rights holder's consent.</li>
    <li>California has required consent for a deceased personality's digital replica since January 1, 2025, and from the same date a vague digital replica clause in an unrepresented performer's contract is unenforceable.</li>
    <li>Utah requires a business to say it is using generative AI when a consumer clearly asks. That governs conversations, not ad creative.</li>
  </ul>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Hawaii's act allows damages of up to 25,000 dollars per advertisement, or actual damages, plus punitive damages where malice is proven. It took effect on approval.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Hawaii Act 247 (HB 2137, CD1), approved July 14, 2026, act text and the governor's message read October 18, 2026.</p>

  <p>Political and issue ads run on separate election laws, state by state. California's makes a political committee's ad with AI-made media carry the line "Ad generated or substantially altered using artificial intelligence." Two other California election deepfake laws were blocked in federal court in August 2025. Check the election code of every state an issue ad runs in.</p>

  <p>The consent paperwork for a real person's face or voice is its own subject, and <a href="/resources/insights/ai-avatars-brand-content">AI avatars in brand content</a> covers it.</p>

  <h2>What do Meta, TikTok, YouTube and LinkedIn ask for?</h2>

  <p>Platform rules aren't law, but they are enforced first: a rejected ad, a removed post. Each network treats organic posts and paid ads separately, and the paid side tends to be stricter.</p>

