  <blockquote class="evidence">TikTok allows significantly edited and AI-generated content in ads only with its AIGC label or "a clear disclaimer, caption, watermark, or sticker" of the advertiser's own, and warns: "If we identify AI-generated content that has not been disclosed, your ad will be rejected or restricted."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: TikTok Ads policy, Misleading and false content, last updated April 2026, read October 18, 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Meta lets advertisers self-disclose AI use on ads delivered to places whose rules call for it, and lists California and New York among them. The label then sits next to Sponsored instead of inside a menu.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Meta Business Help Center, About AI info on ads created or edited with generative AI tools, read October 18, 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Google says its AI label setting "doesn't guarantee your compliance with specific regulations."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Google Ads Help, AI label setting, read October 18, 2026.</p>

  <p>Remember that last line in New York. A platform label tucked behind a menu is not obviously a conspicuous disclosure in the ad, so where a statute applies, put the disclosure in the creative and set the platform label too.</p>

