  <h2>What should a brand keep for every AI-made asset?</h2>

  <p>The statutes ask what you knew and whether you had consent; the platforms ask whether you disclosed. A short record per asset answers both:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>the engine, its version and the date the asset was made</li>
    <li>the prompt, and every source file that went in</li>
    <li>consent on file for any real person's face or voice</li>
    <li>whether a synthetic performer appears, and whether prominently</li>
    <li>who approved which version, and when</li>
    <li>which disclosure went where: the line in the creative, the platform setting, the ad account's self-disclosure</li>
    <li>the states and platforms the asset runs in</li>
    <li>whether the delivered file still carries its provenance data</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The hubStudio app keeps part of this on its own. History keeps every render with its prompt and engine, and Validation keeps who approved which version on a thread nobody can delete. When our studio makes the work, through the <a href="/services/design/ad-creative">ad creative design service</a> for instance, it records provenance per asset. <a href="/resources/insights/disclosure-audit-trail-per-asset">The AI disclosure audit trail per asset</a> sets out the full field list, and the <a href="/resources/copyright-and-ai">Copyright and AI resource</a> covers who owns what.</p>

  <h2>Questions brands ask about AI disclosure in US ads</h2>

  <h3>Do I have to disclose AI-generated content in US ads?</h3>

  <p>Not as a general rule, since no federal law requires it. New York requires a disclosure when an ad has a synthetic performer, and California will from January 1, 2027, when one appears prominently. The FTC expects one when an AI asset would otherwise mislead, as with a generated "customer." TikTok ads and election ads on Meta and Google need their own labels.</p>

  <h3>What does the FTC say about AI in advertising?</h3>

  <p>That the old rules apply. Section 5 bans ads that leave a false net impression, however they were made. The Endorsement Guides reach virtual endorsers and expect real consumers or a disclosure. The reviews rule bans fake reviews, AI-written ones included. The AI cases the FTC has announced went after false claims about what AI products can do.</p>

  <h3>What is New York's synthetic performer disclosure law?</h3>

  <p>General Business Law 396-b, in force since June 9, 2026. Whoever produces or creates an ad must conspicuously disclose a synthetic performer, a generated human who is not any identifiable real person, when they know one is in it. Audio ads and ads for films, shows and games are exempt. Fines are 1,000 dollars, then 5,000 dollars per violation.</p>

  <h3>Does California require labels on AI-generated images?</h3>

  <p>Not from brands. Its AI Transparency Act makes engine makers embed a hidden disclosure in what their systems create, and since September 30, 2026, that holds whatever the system's size. A brand's visible label comes from a different law, from January 1, 2027, when an ad prominently features a synthetic performer, a synthetic voice included.</p>

  <h3>Do Instagram, TikTok and YouTube label AI content automatically?</h3>

  <p>Often. TikTok labels content made with its AI effects or carrying C2PA Content Credentials, and the label can't be removed. YouTube labels content made with its own tools, carrying C2PA data or caught by detection. Meta labels AI content it detects and, since June 2026, ads made with third-party AI tools. Creators still have to disclose realistic content themselves.</p>

  <h3>Can I use AI-generated reviews or testimonials?</h3>

  <p>Not if they pass as real. The FTC's reviews rule bans reviews and testimonials that misrepresent that the reviewer exists, and the agency says AI-generated fake reviews are covered. A knowing violation can cost up to 53,088 dollars each. A generated spokesperson who isn't presented as a customer is a different case, though New York's and California's synthetic performer rules still apply.</p>

  <h3>What records should a brand keep for AI-made ads?</h3>

  <p>For each asset: the engine and version, the prompt and source files, consent for any real face or voice, whether a synthetic performer appears, who approved which version, which disclosure went where, and the states and platforms it ran in. New York's rule turns on what you knew, and the record is how you show it.</p>

